"""
The site's icon set, as data.

Every icon in the design build is drawn the same way: one stroke path and a
second, slightly offset "echo" path that the CSS fades behind it. The echo is
what gives the set its hand-drawn look, and it is unique per icon, so an icon
is a *pair* of paths rather than a single shape.

The names are the design build's own (``green-spark-website/assets/img/icons``),
so a developer looking for the handshake can find it by that name in both
places. The echo path is not in those files — it was only ever in the page
markup — so these pairs are read off the templates, which are what the golden
HTML harness holds us to.

Why a library in code rather than an upload or a markup field:

* An icon is part of the design, not part of the words. Letting an editor paste
  SVG into a content field would mean trusting editor markup on every page,
  which is exactly what the strict CSP and the parity harness exist to avoid.
* The same handful of icons is reused across the site -- the people icon
  appears five times, the spanner three -- so storing paths on each content row
  would mean many copies of one drawing, and correcting one copy would leave
  the rest behind.

So a content row stores a *name* from ``CHOICES`` and the template asks the row
for ``icon_path`` and ``icon_echo``. An unknown or empty name yields empty
strings, and ``IconMixin`` renders nothing at all rather than an empty ``<svg>``
— content that could be missing has to degrade, not break the page.
"""

from dataclasses import dataclass


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Icon:
    """One icon: what to call it in the console, and its two paths."""

    label: str
    path: str
    echo: str


